            {
              "id": "88d4a12c-5f37-3357-8670-dff91611beaa",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the use of weak or non-cryptographic random number generators can lead to vulnerabilities. This is because these generators can produce predictable values, which can be exploited by an attacker to guess the next value that will be generated. This can lead to a variety of security issues, such as session prediction, password cracking, and other forms of information leakage.\n\nIn the given code snippet, `rand()` is a weak random number generator as it uses a linear congruential generator and can be easily predicted if the state of the generator is known.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a strong cryptographic random number generator that produces less predictable values. In C++, you can use functions provided by libraries like `<random>` which is a part of the C++ Standard Library.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code using the `<random>` library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::uniform_int_distribution<> dis(0, 16);\n\nhb[i] = dis(gen);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe fixed code example requires the `<random>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS148264",
                  "88d4a12c-5f37-3357-8670-dff91611beaa"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2dd83b74-cd26-3503-8fd1-b4b390b95b2d",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the use of weak or non-cryptographic random number generators can lead to vulnerabilities. This is because these generators can produce predictable values, which can be exploited by an attacker. For instance, the `rand()` function in C++ is a weak random number generator as it uses a deterministic algorithm. This means that if an attacker can guess or determine the seed value used by `rand()`, they can predict the sequence of random numbers that will be generated.\n\nIn the provided code snippet `ha[i] = rand() % 17`, the `rand()` function is used to generate a random number. However, since `rand()` is a weak random number generator, this code is vulnerable.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a strong cryptographic random number generator instead of `rand()`. In C++, you can use functions provided by libraries such as `<random>` which provides a variety of non-deterministic random number generators.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code using the `<random>` library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::uniform_int_distribution<> dis(0, 16);\n\nha[i] = dis(gen);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe fixed code example requires the `<random>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Insecure Randomness](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Insecure_Randomness)\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS148264",
                  "2dd83b74-cd26-3503-8fd1-b4b390b95b2d"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "b5ef277b-4b3a-3ff4-8d20-baf6a02330d9",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the use of weak or non-cryptographic random number generators can lead to vulnerabilities. This is because these generators can produce predictable values, which can be exploited by an attacker. For instance, the `rand()` function in C++ is a weak random number generator as it uses a deterministic algorithm. This means that if an attacker can guess or determine the seed value used by `rand()`, they can predict the sequence of random numbers that will be generated.\n\nIn the provided code snippet `ha[i] = rand() % 17`, the `rand()` function is used to generate a random number. However, since `rand()` is a weak random number generator, this code is vulnerable.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a strong cryptographic random number generator instead of `rand()`. In C++, you can use functions provided by libraries such as `<random>` which provides a variety of non-deterministic random number generators.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a fixed version of the code using the `<random>` library:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::uniform_int_distribution<> dis(0, 16);\n\nha[i] = dis(gen);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe fixed code example requires the `<random>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Insecure Randomness](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Insecure_Randomness)\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS148264",
                  "b5ef277b-4b3a-3ff4-8d20-baf6a02330d9"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "273d564e-4ac8-3597-aef5-fc03bdf6639e",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, using system time or a static value for seeding randomness can lead to predictable results, which is a security vulnerability. This is because the seed value determines the sequence of random numbers that will be generated. If an attacker can predict or know the seed value, they can predict the sequence of random numbers, which can lead to various security issues, such as predictable session identifiers, predictable cryptographic keys, etc.\n\nIn the provided code example, `srand(1)` is used to seed the random number generator with a static value of 1. This means that every time the program is run, the same sequence of random numbers will be generated, which is predictable and hence insecure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a truly random value to seed the random number generator. This can be achieved by using a high-quality source of randomness provided by the operating system, such as `/dev/urandom` on Unix-like systems or `CryptGenRandom` on Windows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\n```\n\nIn this code, `std::random_device` is used to generate a non-deterministic random number, which is then used to seed the Mersenne Twister random number generator (`std::mt19937`).\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<random>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-330: Use of Insufficiently Random Values](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/330.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS149435"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "94a6f45b-6c0a-3d53-a9e5-4f28e0b52532",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the use of non-cryptographic or weak random number generators can lead to vulnerabilities. This is because these generators can produce predictable values, which can be exploited by an attacker. The `rand()` function in C++ is a non-cryptographic random number generator, which means it is not suitable for generating random numbers in a security context.\n\nThe specific vulnerability sink in the provided code is the use of `rand() % 17`. This code generates a random number between 0 and 16. However, because `rand()` is a weak random number generator, the output can be predictable, making it a potential security risk.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should use a cryptographic random number generator instead of `rand()`. In C++, you can use functions from the `<random>` library, such as `std::random_device` and `std::mt19937`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::uniform_int_distribution<> dis(0, 16);\n\nhx[i] = dis(gen);\n```\n\nIn this code, `std::random_device` is used to seed the Mersenne Twister engine (`std::mt19937`), which is a cryptographic random number generator. Then, `std::uniform_int_distribution<>` is used to generate a random number between 0 and 16.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<random>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS148264",
                  "94a6f45b-6c0a-3d53-a9e5-4f28e0b52532"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "37bb2bbd-5502-34ba-b435-645c30d3c690",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the use of non-cryptographic or weak random number generators can lead to vulnerabilities. This is because these generators can produce predictable values, which can be exploited by an attacker. The `rand()` function in C++ is a non-cryptographic random number generator, which means it is not suitable for generating random numbers in a security context.\n\nThe specific vulnerability sink in the provided code is the use of `rand() % 17`. This code generates a random number between 0 and 16. However, because `rand()` is a weak random number generator, the output can be predictable, making it a potential security risk.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should use a cryptographic random number generator instead of `rand()`. In C++, you can use functions from the `<random>` library, such as `std::random_device` and `std::mt19937`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::uniform_int_distribution<> dis(0, 16);\n\nhx[i] = dis(gen);\n```\n\nIn this code, `std::random_device` is used to seed the Mersenne Twister engine (`std::mt19937`), which is a cryptographic random number generator. Then, `std::uniform_int_distribution<>` is used to generate a random number between 0 and 16.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<random>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
