            {
              "id": "c8f39647-3e8f-3523-9c28-12741441caaa",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the function atof() is used, which converts a string to a double. This function does not check for overflow or underflow, and does not report errors, making it a potential security risk.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using C library functions that do not perform bounds checking or error reporting. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library. In this case, you can use std::stod() function which throws an exception if the conversion cannot be performed or if the converted value would fall out of the range of the result type.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the atof() function with std::stod(). Here is how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string>\n\nint main(int argc, char* argv[]) {\n    // ...\n    std::stod(argv[++i]);\n    // ...\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<string>`: This is a standard C++ library for using string data type.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "479e9e0f-991c-31d9-83b3-aa130626a247",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to arbitrary code execution or denial of service. In this case, the function `atoi()` is used, which can lead to integer overflow or underflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using unsafe C standard library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives that perform bounds checking or other forms of validation to prevent buffer overflows and other vulnerabilities. In the case of `atoi()`, consider using `strtol()` or `sscanf()`, which provide error checking.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `atoi(argv[++i])` with a safer alternative. Here is an example using `strtol()`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib> // for strtol\n#include <cerrno>  // for errno\n\nchar *end;\nlong val = strtol(argv[++i], &end, 10);\n\nif (end == argv[i] || *end != '\\0' || errno == ERANGE) {\n    // handle conversion error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the C standard library, which is typically included with the C++ compiler.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "452d29b4-590a-3359-8cd5-ece08807e4b0",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to arbitrary code execution or denial of service. In this case, the function `atoi()` is used, which can lead to integer overflow or underflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using unsafe C standard library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives that perform bounds checking or other forms of validation to prevent buffer overflows and other vulnerabilities. In the case of `atoi()`, consider using `strtol()` or `sscanf()`, which provide error checking.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `atoi(argv[++i])` with a safer alternative. Here is an example using `strtol()`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib> // for strtol\n#include <cerrno>  // for errno\n\nchar *end;\nlong val = strtol(argv[++i], &end, 10);\n\nif (end == argv[i] || *end != '\\0' || errno == ERANGE) {\n    // handle conversion error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the C standard library, which is typically included with the C++ compiler.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
