                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "439fd2c3-7715-3cc0-957b-6da4abfa2d33",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of insecure C library functions in C++ code. These functions are often prone to buffer overflow attacks, format string vulnerabilities, or integer overflows. In this case, the function atof() is used, which converts a string to a double. This function does not check for overflow or underflow, and does not report errors, making it a potential security risk.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using C library functions that do not perform bounds checking or error reporting. Instead, use safer alternatives provided by the C++ Standard Library. In this case, you can use std::stod() function which throws an exception if the conversion cannot be performed or if the converted value would fall out of the range of the result type.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the atof() function with std::stod(). Here is how you can do it:\n\n```cpp\n#include <string>\n\nint main(int argc, char* argv[]) {\n    // ...\n    std::stod(argv[++i]);\n    // ...\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library:\n\n- `<string>`: This is a standard C++ library for using string data type.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++](https://owasp.org/www-pdf-archive/OWASP_SCP_v2.pdf)\n- [CWE-676: Use of Potentially Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/676.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "4623ccf8-c5f5-3526-bb59-78bdce63a133",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to arbitrary code execution or denial of service. In this case, the function `atoi()` is used, which can lead to integer overflow or underflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using unsafe C standard library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives that perform bounds checking or other forms of validation to prevent buffer overflows and other vulnerabilities. In the case of `atoi()`, consider using `strtol()` or `sscanf()`, which provide error checking.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `atoi(argv[++i])` with a safer alternative. Here is an example using `strtol()`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib> // for strtol\n#include <cerrno>  // for errno\n\nchar *end;\nlong val = strtol(argv[++i], &end, 10);\n\nif (end == argv[i] || *end != '\\0' || errno == ERANGE) {\n    // handle conversion error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the C standard library, which is typically included with the C++ compiler.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "09aca5dc-4194-3f48-9087-8d9e5748cb86",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to arbitrary code execution or denial of service. In this case, the function `atoi()` is used, which can lead to integer overflow or underflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using unsafe C standard library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives that perform bounds checking or other forms of validation to prevent buffer overflows and other vulnerabilities. In the case of `atoi()`, consider using `strtol()` or `sscanf()`, which provide error checking.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `atoi(argv[++i])` with a safer alternative. Here is an example using `strtol()`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib> // for strtol\n#include <cerrno>  // for errno\n\nchar *end;\nlong val = strtol(argv[++i], &end, 10);\n\nif (end == argv[i] || *end != '\\0' || errno == ERANGE) {\n    // handle conversion error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the C standard library, which is typically included with the C++ compiler.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS154189"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "183cf430-ce50-3e9f-9df3-ba1d5bd28f42",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the vulnerability \"Avoid Using Non-Cryptographic or Weak Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of functions like `rand()` to generate random numbers. These functions are not suitable for generating random numbers in a security context because they are predictable and can be easily reproduced. This can lead to various security issues, such as predictability of passwords, tokens, or other security-related random values.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a strong cryptographic random number generator. In C++, you can use functions provided by libraries like `<random>` which are designed to produce high-quality random numbers suitable for cryptographic use.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `rand()` function with a strong cryptographic random number generator. Here is an example of how you can do this in C++:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::uniform_int_distribution<> dis(0, 2);\n\nhx[i] = hx_gold[i] = dis(gen);\n```\n\nIn this code, `std::random_device` is a uniformly-distributed integer random number generator that produces non-deterministic random numbers. `std::mt19937` is a Mersenne Twister pseudo-random generator of 32-bit numbers with a state size of 19937 bits. `std::uniform_int_distribution` produces random integers in a range [a, b].\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<random>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS148264",
                  "183cf430-ce50-3e9f-9df3-ba1d5bd28f42"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "2fd6c499-2206-3989-bc4e-5cbd5aa8984f",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, using system time or a static value for seeding randomness can lead to predictable results, which is a security vulnerability. This is because the seed value determines the sequence of random numbers that will be generated. If an attacker can predict or know the seed value, they can predict the sequence of random numbers, which can lead to various security issues, such as predictable session identifiers, predictable cryptographic keys, etc.\n\nIn the provided code example, `srand(1)` is used to seed the random number generator with a static value of 1. This means that every time the program is run, the same sequence of random numbers will be generated, which is predictable and hence insecure.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, use a truly random value to seed the random number generator. This can be achieved by using a high-quality source of randomness provided by the operating system, such as `/dev/urandom` on Unix-like systems or `CryptGenRandom` on Windows.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code example:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\n```\n\nIn this code, `std::random_device` is used to generate a non-deterministic random number, which is then used to seed the Mersenne Twister random number generator (`std::mt19937`).\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<random>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-330: Use of Insufficiently Random Values](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/330.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "tags": [
                  "DS149435"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "170c44ac-53c6-32ce-b315-a0fc4f45de75",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\n\"Detected Prohibited C Function\" vulnerability refers to the use of certain C standard library functions that are considered unsafe due to their potential to cause buffer overflows, format string vulnerabilities, or other issues that can lead to arbitrary code execution or denial of service. In this case, the function `atoi()` is used, which can lead to integer overflow or underflow.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nAvoid using unsafe C standard library functions. Instead, use safer alternatives that perform bounds checking or other forms of validation to prevent buffer overflows and other vulnerabilities. In the case of `atoi()`, consider using `strtol()` or `sscanf()`, which provide error checking.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nReplace the `atoi(argv[++i])` with a safer alternative. Here is an example using `strtol()`:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstdlib> // for strtol\n#include <cerrno>  // for errno\n\nchar *end;\nlong val = strtol(argv[++i], &end, 10);\n\nif (end == argv[i] || *end != '\\0' || errno == ERANGE) {\n    // handle conversion error\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the C standard library, which is typically included with the C++ compiler.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/242.html)"
